
1. No trap builtin: a handler is a function
--------------------------------------------------------------
function on_int --on-signal INT
    echo "on_int  -- not stopping" >> log
end

fish defined all four without a word, including the last. Sending the
first three, a third of a second apart:

$ cat log
on_int  -- not stopping
on_term -- not stopping

fish ships a `trap` function for compatibility, and it is a thin wrapper
that defines exactly this kind of function for you. There is no builtin
underneath it.

2. Three signals sent, two handlers ran
--------------------------------------------------------------
SIGINT and SIGTERM arrived. SIGQUIT did not, and its handler was
declared in the same breath as the other two. fish does not deliver
SIGQUIT to a --on-signal handler -- so Ctrl-\, which is the key that
ends a program whose SIGINT handler has taken over, does nothing to a
fish script either. That is fish's decision, not the kernel's.

3. And the one no shell can reach
--------------------------------------------------------------
$ cat log
on_int  -- not stopping
on_term -- not stopping

Unchanged. SIGKILL and SIGSTOP are acted on by the kernel before any
process sees them, so no shell has anything to be wrong about -- fish
accepting --on-signal KILL is the same silence as bash accepting
trap ... KILL.

4. The status a signal leaves behind
--------------------------------------------------------------
$ fish -c "kill -INT %self"; echo $status
130

$ fish -c "kill -KILL %self"; echo $status
137

fish spells it $status rather than $?, and the arithmetic is everyone
else's: 128 + the signal number. 130 is somebody's Ctrl-C, 137 is a
kill -9 or the out-of-memory killer, 143 is a shutdown that asked first.

5. fish has no kill builtin, and it shows
--------------------------------------------------------------
$ kill -l KILL
9

bash and zsh both have a kill builtin that takes a signal name. fish
does not, so this is /bin/kill -- and the two columns of this page are
two different /bin/kill programs. Use the status arithmetic above
instead; it is the same on both.

6. A background job's SIGINT, where fish sides with zsh
--------------------------------------------------------------
$ cat log
catcher: caught SIGINT

It caught it. Every shell here starts a background child with SIGINT and
SIGQUIT set to ignored, and POSIX says a signal ignored at startup can
never be trapped afterwards. bash honours that and its handler never
runs; fish and zsh override it and theirs do. The child's shell decides
and the parent's does not -- which is the section to re-read when a
wrapper script goes quiet after somebody changed its first line.
